nment, and Poindexter's solid fundraising have shifted the race; raters cluster at Tossup with a Lean D tilt and markets price the challenger well ahead. Partisan polls from August support a modest Democratic edge, though their age and sponsorship warrant caution.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling in October, or new developments in the allegations against Miller, could move this back toward a pure tossup or further toward Poindexter.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 56 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Max Miller (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Ohio's 7th District was drawn as a Republican-leaning seat, and Max Miller began the cycle as the favorite by incumbency alone. But abuse allegations against Miller, which he has denied, and a combative exchange of attack ads have changed the race's character, and national analysts now treat the seat as one of the House's most competitive. Most raters call it a Tossup, while two rate it a Democratic flip opportunity.\n\nBrian Poindexter, a former Brook Park council member, has raised over $600,000 and drawn outside support, while Miller retains a large cash advantage. The limited polling available, all from partisan-sponsored pollsters in June through early August, shows Poindexter leading by margins ranging from one to sixteen points. Prediction markets lean strongly toward the Democrat. The race remains genuinely competitive, with affordability and the scandal dominating the closing argument.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6543, "margin": 2.32}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.7013, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7273, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7059, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6482, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6734, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.7173, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6921, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem":